# TLS server, CHUNKING reception with TLS shutdown mid-chunk
gnutls
#
# A TLS session that goes away while a BDAT chunk is still being read leaves the
# server holding readers, and a transfer buffer, belonging to a session that has
# ceased to exist.  Reading has to stop there.  Going on would either reach into
# the released buffer or read the rest of the connection in clear, on the socket
# the peer negotiated encryption for.
#
# Four client sessions run in turn against the one daemon.  The first is the
# reproducer.  The three after it are negative controls for the other positions
# the same teardown can arrive at, and each asserts the same thing from a
# different angle: whatever the peer offers in clear afterwards is not read as a
# continuation of the session that has gone.
#
# Note for running this by hand: the daemon under test has to be the GnuTLS
# build.  Hand that binary to runtest and leave --tls off - that is the case in
# which the binary runtest was given is used at both ends, as $exim_client and as
# $exim_server alike.  Given --tls, runtest works in its cross-library mode
# instead: it picks the harness client program, and it also pairs the two
# library-specific builds against each other, so "--tls gnutls" leaves the
# OpenSSL build answering as $exim_server - and that build never frees the
# transfer buffer this test is about.  Test numbers spanning more than one script
# directory have to be asked for as a range, or one batch per directory.
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
# Session 1 - the reproducer.
#
# Chunk arithmetic - 85 declared, 84 sent, so one byte is still outstanding when
# the session goes.  The ">>> " prefix suppresses the trailing CRLF the client
# would otherwise add, so the payload is exactly the text after it:
#
#     To: Susan@random.com CRLF      22
#     From: Sam@random.com CRLF      22   (44)
#     CRLF, ending the header block   2   (46)
#     body-start-                    11   (57)
#     27 x A                         27   (84)
#
# Both halves of that shape are load-bearing.  The header block has to be
# complete, so that reception is past the header lines and into the body, which is
# where a released transfer buffer is read and written.  And the chunk has to be
# short, so that the session goes away with the transfer still owed data: a
# reader which treats what arrived as the whole of the message would accept it.
#
# The reply is matched in full rather than as just "421" because the receive
# timeout answers with a 421 of its own, and a loose match would be satisfied by
# the very behaviour this test exists to reject - a server that keeps reading the
# plain socket has nothing to read there, and says so when the timeout expires.
#
# Nothing is offered in clear after the shutdown, so the server has nothing
# unconsumed to reset over and its close is clean.  That is what lets the end of
# file be asserted here, and asserting it is half the point: a server which
# answered 421 and then left the socket usable would pass on the reply alone.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 85 LAST
>>> To: Susan@random.com\r\nFrom: Sam@random.com\r\n\r\nbody-start-AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
stoptls
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
???*
****
#
# Session 2 - the same teardown, and then the byte itself.
#
# Identical up to the shutdown, after which one byte is pushed in clear.  That is
# the byte the chunk is still owed, and following the released buffer is exactly
# what a reader would do with it, so this is the shortest path there is to the
# original fault.  The answer has to be the same lost-connection reply as above:
# the byte is not what completes the chunk, and no message may be accepted.  A
# server that reads it answers "250- 85 byte chunk" instead, having taken a chunk
# as complete on the strength of a byte that arrived in clear.
#
# Unlike session 1 this leaves something unconsumed in the socket, so the close
# that follows the reply is a reset rather than an orderly one.  The reply is
# therefore all that is asserted here; the mainlog carries the rest, and shows
# the same lost-connection record and no acceptance.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 85 LAST
>>> To: Susan@random.com\r\nFrom: Sam@random.com\r\n\r\nbody-start-AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
stoptls
>>> X
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
****
#
# Session 3 - teardown part-way through a command line.
#
# No transaction is open, so nothing is being read for a message; what is
# incomplete is the command line itself, sent without its terminator.  The rest
# of it is then supplied in clear, along with a further command.
#
# Joining the two halves would be the worst of the readings available: half a
# command carrying whatever standing the session conferred, completed by a peer
# that no longer has it - a server that joins them answers "250 OK" to the address
# so assembled.  The input is finished instead, and the reply says so.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
>>> MAIL FROM:<someone@some.
stoptls
>>> domain>\r\nRCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>\r\n
??? 421 testhost.test.ex lost input connection
???*
****
#
# Session 4 - teardown between commands, which is the one position at which the
# connection carries on.
#
# Nothing is in flight, so there is nothing to truncate and the remains of the
# connection are handed to a plain dialogue - which is what an Exim client asking
# for no TLS proxy does, and what test 1157 requires of this server.  Carrying on
# is not the same as carrying anything over, and that is what is asserted here:
# the socket now answers only what RFC 3207 section 4 leaves a server requiring
# TLS able to answer, and refuses everything else with the 530 it prescribes.
#
# A transaction, an address lookup and an offer of credentials are each refused,
# which between them cover accepting a message, exposing an address and
# authenticating - the three things the ended session could otherwise have been
# spent on.  Three refusals is also the most that can be asserted in one session,
# since a fourth passes smtp_max_synprot_errors and the connection is dropped.
#
# A fresh greeting is then answered, and its extension set is asserted in full:
# STARTTLS is offered again, which is both the proof that the state behind the
# advertisement was recomputed rather than remembered and the handoff that test
# 1157 depends on.  Answering it is where this session stops: the harness client
# cannot start a second session once it has stopped the first, because stoptls
# takes the library's global state down with it (src/client.c), and it offers no
# way to choose a protocol version or a server name for a session either.  So the
# second handshake itself is left to test 1157, which drives that sequence from an
# Exim client, and a successor session differing from its predecessor in version
# or server name is not expressible in this harness at all.
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
stoptls
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 530 Must issue a STARTTLS command first
VRFY postmaster
??? 530 Must issue a STARTTLS command first
AUTH PLAIN
??? 530 Must issue a STARTTLS command first
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
QUIT
??? 221
****
millisleep 500
